# Accepted gap: a record DECONSTRUCTION invokes the component accessors, and the engine emits no
# call site for them.
#
# `if (n instanceof Pair(Leaf a, Node ignored))` compiles to invocations of Pair#left() and
# Pair#right() — javac has to call them to obtain the components to bind. The edges are real rather
# than plumbing: a record may declare an accessor with a body of its own, so the deconstruction can
# dispatch into user code.
#
# They are missing for a structural reason rather than a resolution one — there is no expression in
# the IR to hang them on. Every call site the engine knows comes from a java_expression row, and the
# accessor calls are written nowhere; they would have to be synthesised, one per component of every
# record pattern. That is a different mechanism from the binding typing this case exercises, which
# is why it is recorded here rather than folded in.
#
# Everything WRITTEN in the source resolves: oracle=12 engine=6 agree=6 extra=0.
#
# It cannot rot: if the engine starts emitting them, these lines report NOW-FIXED and fail.
probe.Patterns#bothComponents -> probe.Pair#left()
probe.Patterns#bothComponents -> probe.Pair#right()
probe.Patterns#deconstruct -> probe.Pair#left()
probe.Patterns#deconstruct -> probe.Pair#right()
probe.Patterns#inSwitch -> probe.Pair#left()
probe.Patterns#inSwitch -> probe.Pair#right()
